// frontseat.plugin.v1alpha1: what a plugin is asked of releasing.
//
// The host builds the release graph: which entities are units, which
// units depend on which, what each unit's members are, and for each unit
// its last release and the commits since it that changed its members'
// files. It knows nothing of how a version moves or which branches
// release. Those are plugins', in two roles, and a plugin may serve either
// beside its others:
//
//   - A branching strategy (Branch) says what a branch releases: whether
//     it releases at all, a release or a prerelease, and held to which
//     line. GitHub Flow, GitLab
//     Flow's release branches and Release Flow are three such.
//   - A versioning strategy (Version) says what a unit's next version is,
//     and why, from its commits. Conventional commits is one such.
//
// A unit names its strategies in spec.frontseat.release, inherited like
// every release setting; a strategy's own options are its plugin's
// options, declared and checked as every option is.
//
// Branch and Version read nothing through the host: what they are told is
// all they know, so the same question has the same answer, and the host
// can keep it. The host asks Branch once for each unit, then Version. It
// checks every answer: a version must be above the last release, on the
// branch's line, and tagged on no other commit; a unit that holds is told
// nothing more.

